  > nohup command &
 
means:

“Run command in the background and make sure it keeps running even if I close the terminal.”

What nohup does with output

When you run:

nohup command &
nohup detaches the command from the terminal
It redirects output away from the terminal

Specifically:

If stdout (standard output) is not already redirected, nohup writes it to:

nohup.out
That file is created:
In the current working directory, if writable
Otherwise in your home directory (~/nohup.out)
stderr (errors) is also redirected to the same place by default

👉 So yes:

It creates nohup.out in the current directory and sends output there instead of the terminal.


comparison with better alternatives:


1 -tmux (or screen)

These create a persistent terminal session.

Example flow:

tmux
python script.py
# detach with Ctrl+B then D

You can later reconnect:

tmux attach

Best for: interactive or long-running tasks


2 - systemd services

Used to run programs as managed background services.

Best for: production apps, servers


